<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Two Capitals</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1328">1328</a>–<a href="/years/1332">1332</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Tianli Incident</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Two Capitals, or Tianli Incident, was a war of succession in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> fought in 1328 between forces based at the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>) and at the summer capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a> after the death of the emperor Yesün Temür. At Shangdu his favourite retainer Dawlat Shah installed Yesün Temür's son Ragibagh, while at Khanbaliq the Qipchaq commander El Temür enthroned Tugh Temür, a son of Khayishan. The Khanbaliq side held the richer central provinces; its allies surrounded Shangdu on 14 November and the loyalists there surrendered the next day. It was the bloodiest succession in Yuan history, and the last loyalists did not give up until 1332.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reigns grew short after Kublai’s death in 1294: a group of princes murdered Emperor Gegeen in 1323, and in 1328 a civil war, the <b>War of the Two Capitals</b>, decided the throne.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
